= Getting Started: Your First Agent

This book has taught you the principles. Now here's the practical bit: which tool do you actually open on Monday morning?

This is the most perishable chapter in the book, on purpose. The rest of the book is written to survive the next product launch; this one is a snapshot of the tools as they stood in September 2026. Some of it will be out of date within months. When it is, trust the principles and check the vendors' own websites, not this page.

== The Three Paths

=== Path 1: Chat Assistants (Free to Low Cost)

*What:* A conversation interface where you type instructions and the agent responds with text, analysis, or files. Since the first edition, these have grown hands: most can now search the web, work with files you upload, remember your preferences, and — if you switch on _connectors_ — reach into your email, calendar, and documents.

*Best for:* Writing, analysis, brainstorming, drafting reports, creating templates — the kind of work from the Monday report walkthrough.

*Options:*
- *Claude* (claude.ai) — Free tier available, with paid plans for heavier use. This is the agent behind this book.
- *ChatGPT* (chatgpt.com) — Free tier available, with paid plans. Widely used, strong general capabilities.
- *Gemini* (gemini.google.com) — Free tier available. Integrates well with Google Workspace.

*How to start:* Sign up. Type a task. See what happens. Start with something low-stakes — summarise a document, draft an email, analyse a small dataset. Before you switch on any connectors, re-read the chapter on hidden instructions. Start with one, read-only if you can.

=== Path 2: Coding Agents (For Building Software)

*What:* Agents that can write, edit, and run code directly. They work inside your project, create files, and build working software from your instructions.

*Best for:* Building apps like the window quoting tool. Creating websites, automating workflows, building tools.

*Options:*
- *Claude Code* — Anthropic's coding agent. It started as a terminal tool, and now also runs in a desktop app and on the web, where it can work on a project in the cloud and hand back the result. Included with Claude's paid plans.
- *Codex* — OpenAI's equivalent, available from ChatGPT's paid plans, with both a local version and cloud tasks.
- *Cursor* (cursor.com) — A code editor with a built-in agent. Feels like a normal editor, but the agent can write and change code alongside you. Free tier available.
- *GitHub Copilot* — Built into VS Code and GitHub. It now includes an agent you can assign a task to on GitHub, which comes back with a pull request.

*How to start:* If you're following the window quoting walkthrough, pick the one whose interface feels least intimidating — for most people that's a desktop app or an editor like Cursor rather than a bare terminal. Open a new, empty project, ask for a plan first, and start describing what you want. It creates the files for you.

=== Path 3: No-Code Automation Platforms

*What:* Visual tools that let you connect agents to services, automate workflows, and build processes without writing code.

*Best for:* Automating business processes. Connecting tools together. The kind of workflows covered in the "Extending the Crew's Reach" chapter.

*Options:*
- *Zapier* (zapier.com) — Connects thousands of apps. Free tier for basic automations.
- *Make* (make.com) — Similar to Zapier, more visual. Free tier available.
- *n8n* (n8n.io) — Open-source, self-hostable. Free to run yourself, cloud plans available.

*How to start:* Pick a simple automation. "When I get an email from a specific client, summarise it and post it to our team Slack channel." Build it in Zapier or Make. See how it feels. And notice the trifecta question: this automation reads outside email and posts somewhere. Keep it away from anything private.

== What You'll Need

*For chat-based agents:* A web browser. That's it.

*For coding agents:* The web and desktop versions need little more than an account. The terminal versions need a computer with a terminal (Mac or Linux is easiest) and Git, and each tool's install page will tell you what else it wants. If those words make you nervous, ask a chat assistant to walk you through the setup — "How do I install Git on my Mac?" is a perfectly good first prompt.

*For no-code platforms:* A web browser and accounts for the services you want to connect.

== The First Week

Here's a realistic schedule for getting started:

*Day 1:* Sign up for Claude or ChatGPT. Ask it to summarise a long email or document. Ask it to draft a reply. Get comfortable with the conversation. Then write a few lines of custom instructions about who you are and how you like things done, so tomorrow's conversation starts from there.

*Day 2:* Try the Monday report exercise from the book. Export some real data, provide an example of what you want, and see what the agent produces.

*Day 3:* Ask the agent to help with something you've been putting off — a presentation outline, a project plan, a comparison of three options for a decision you need to make.

*Day 4:* Try giving the agent raw materials instead of descriptions. Notice the difference in output quality. Then try a bigger task and ask for a plan before it starts.

*Day 5:* Set up one small automation. An email template. A recurring report. Something that saves you fifteen minutes a week.

By Friday, you'll have a working sense of what agents can do, where they struggle, and how to get the best out of them. Everything else is iteration.

== The Cost

Most people can get meaningful value from free tiers. The entry-level paid plans — around \$20 a month from each of the big vendors — are worth it if you're using agents daily: the limits are higher, the models are stronger, and you get the coding agents and connectors. Heavy-use plans run from about \$100 to \$200 a month; you'll know if you need one, because you'll keep hitting the limits of the cheaper plan.

For context: \$20 a month is less than a single hour of a freelancer's time. If the agent saves you two hours a week, the maths is obvious.

== One More Thing

Don't wait until you feel ready. You won't. The best way to learn is to open the tool, give it a real task, and see what happens. The first output won't be perfect. That's fine. You iterate. That's literally the point of this entire book.

You have the principles. Now go get your hands dirty.
